# TypeScript conventions

## Types

- `strict` TypeScript. No `any`. Prefer `unknown` + narrow at boundaries.
- Branded / nominal types for money and IDs (`Msat`, `Rwf`, `UsdtMicros`, payment id).
- Shared domain types live in `packages/shared`: do not duplicate enums/status strings.
- Prefer `readonly` and `as const` for config and status unions.

## Modules

- Named exports. One primary export per file when practical.
- Colocate: `feature/handler.ts`, `feature/schema.ts`, `feature/service.ts`.
- Import with clear paths; no circular deps between `services/*`.

## Async & errors

```ts
// ❌ BAD
try { await disburse(); } catch (e) {}

// ✅ GOOD
try {
  await disburse();
} catch (e) {
  throw mapMomoError(e);
}
```

- Propagate typed errors (`AppError` with `code`). Map at the edge to HTTP.
- Never swallow. Log once at the boundary with structured fields (no PII dumps).
- `async` functions always either return a value or throw: no mixed “null means error” unless documented in shared Result type.

## Validation

- Validate all external input (HTTP, MoMo, LND) with a schema library (zod or equivalent) at the boundary.
- Trust internal packages only after types prove it.

## Style

- `const` by default; `let` only when reassignment is required.
- Prefer `??` / `?.` over verbose null checks.
- No `console.log` in services: use the shared logger.
